ce, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's assess each:\n\nA: reliability 100% (strong). Decision quality quick correct decisions (good). Crew cooperation positive atmosphere (good). Communication difficulty (weak). Egocentric (maybe weak teamwork). Not willing to further education (weak adaptability). Overall strong reliability but concerns on communication and adaptability.\n\nB: conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (good), good at weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but grumpy and uncooperative (crew cooperation, communication). So strengths but teamwork concerns.\n\nC: anticipates dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision), sees complex connections (technical), spatial vision (technical), leadership qualities (crew cooperation), but not good at taking criticism (adaptability), unorganized (attention). So strong technical and decision, but concerns on organization and feedback.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentration (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but arrogant (crew cooperation), weak leadership (crew). So mixed.\n\nWhich candidate best overall? Need balanced across criteria. A has reliability and crew atmosphere but communication issues. B has stress and technical but cooperation issues. C has technical and decision but organization and criticism issues. D has problem solving and responsibility but arrogance and weak leadership.\n\nProbably Candidate A's reliability and positive crew atmosphere may outweigh communication difficulty? But difficulty communicating ideas could be serious for cockpit. B's uncooperative also serious. C's unorganized could affect safety. D's arrogance also serious.\n\nThus maybe Candidate A is best overall despite communication difficulty, because reliability and positive atmosphere are crucial. However need to consider that difficulty communicating ideas may affect safety-critical communication. But we have no evidence of actual communication failures, just difficulty. Could be mitigated.\n\nThus I will recommend Candidate A, but note concerns.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: there is none yet, but we can say \"Given the facts we have, I propose...\"\n\nWe must include at least one fact. Provide reasoning.\n\nNow produce output."}
